// All the streams of an HTTP/2 session share a single socket. Handing a large
// payload to the session in one write keeps that write queued for a long time,
// and if the client cancels the stream in the meantime the session is left
// with a write it can no longer complete, hanging every other stream on it.
// Writing one HTTP/2 frame at a time, while honoring backpressure, keeps the
// queued data small and lets us stop as soon as the stream goes away.
// 16KiB is the default maximum frame size, see
// https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9113#section-6.5.2
const HTTP2_MAX_CHUNK_SIZE = 16 * 1024

